.\" kwd(1) - Kubernetes workload readiness watchdog
.\" Manual page for kwd
.TH KWD 1 "2026-10-09" "kwd v0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kwd \- Kubernetes workload readiness watchdog
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kwd
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kwd
is a Go CLI that checks the
.B readiness
of the Kubernetes workloads you declare in YAML, in a single pass, and reports
the verdict via its exit code (
.BR 0
= all ready,
.BR 1
= any not-ready or errored).
It uses
.B client-go
directly (no
.BR kubectl
binary) and selects its target cluster by kubeconfig context.
.PP
kwd checks
.B deployment
and
.B statefulset
kinds. With
.IR "interval: 0"
(the default) it runs once and exits
.BR 0 / 1 .
With
.I interval > 0
it runs a serial daemon loop (check, wait, check), prints the readiness
table and notifies only on overall state transitions, and exits
.B 0
on SIGINT/SIGTERM. There is no
.B \-\-daemon
flag; runtime shape is solely
.IR interval .
Slack notifications use a fail-closed webhook env var when configured.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Run a readiness check over declared resources (also the default for bare
.BR kwd ).
.TP
.B analyze
Validate config and print the normalized check plan (no cluster contact).
.TP
.B target
Print the resolved Kubernetes API target (cluster and context name).
.TP
.B notify test
Notification helper (reserved; reports not available in v0.1.0).
.TP
.B version
Print version, commit, build date, and branch.
.TP
.B completion \fIbash|zsh|fish|powershell\fR
Write a shell completion script to stdout.
.PP
Release packages also ship
.B kubectl\-kwd
(same CLI). With that binary on
.BR PATH ,
invoke as
.B kubectl kwd \fIsubcommand\fR
\&.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-config " \fIpath\fR"
Config file path. Default when omitted:
.IR ./kwd.yaml .
.TP
.BR \-\-print\-sample\-config
Write the sample config to stdout and exit (same as
.IR configs/kwd.sample.yml ).
.TP
.BR \-\-interval " \fIseconds\fR"
Override
.I interval
for
.B check
only (seconds;
.B 0
= single-pass). Precedence: YAML →
.B KWD_INTERVAL
→ this flag (flag applies only when set). There is no
.B \-\-daemon
flag.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is a YAML file with these top-level blocks:
.IR cluster ,
.IR kube ,
.IR client ,
.IR resources ,
.IR interval ,
.IR notifications ,
.IR dry_run .
Resource refs use
.IR kind.namespace/name
(e.g.
.IR deployment.default/web ,
.IR statefulset.myns/redis ).
.PP
Environment variables with prefix
.B KWD_
override YAML when loaded (e.g.
.BR KWD_CLIENT_ID ,
.BR KWD_KUBE_CONTEXT ,
.BR KWD_INTERVAL ,
.BR KWD_DRY_RUN ).
The Slack webhook is read from
.BR KWD_SLACK_WEBHOOK
(fail-closed when a Slack sink is declared but the variable is missing).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success \- all declared resources ready (single-pass), or graceful daemon
shutdown (SIGINT/SIGTERM when
.IR "interval > 0" ).
.TP
.B 1
One or more resources not-ready or errored (single-pass only; the daemon
shape does not map last-tick health to the exit code).
.TP
.B 2
Reserved for
.B doctor
RBAC preflight (later release).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kubectl (1)
.PP
